in August of 2018 I released a video  discussing the characters Vegeta and  Goku asking you the audience to decide  which of the two you identify with more  and who you might want to be if given  the choice by an incredibly wide margin  Vegeta was the character chosen so I  asked on Twitter why don't people like  Goku and these were the most common  talking points number one he's  one-dimensional number two he's childish  and number three because of his  character he can be seen as predictable  and boring and while I don't agree at  all with his character being boring or  one-dimensional check out my last video  I can 100% gone bored with the notion  that he's not a relatable character  insofar as it pertained to some large  aspects of his persona and morals which  is why we have the antihero  enter the cheetah  flawed unapologetic when it comes to  getting the job done and oh so relatable  since his debut of the late 80s Vegeta  has become a globally recognized  character and is a favorite for many in  the series but how does one write and  antihero character and how did Akira  Toriyama make a character like Vegeta  that resonated with so many people well  let's take a look  what makes an antihero a lot of the time  nowadays many script writers both for TV  and film create antihero characters that  are just for lack of a better term  one-dimensional Basterds and while a  certain age is part of it it is on a  fundamental level not what makes an  antihero  an antihero anti heroes are great guys  who turned bad for sympathetic reasons  right for sympathetic reasons Michael  Corleone II becomes a horrible man why  he's saving his family right Tony  Soprano is a horrible guy but we like  him why because he wants to save his  family when writing the antihero  character it is important to understand  what makes an antihero and what does not  make an antihero  for example the antihero of the story is  not the villain of the story which means  that Vegeta has not always been the  antihero character he became one when he  was first introduced during the Saiyan  arc of Dragon Ball Z his actions were  seemingly irredeemable it wasn't until  after the fight with Goku did we notice  another side to Vegeta typically in a  movie the antihero is such from the  beginning a central character that lacks  conventional heroic attributes while  they may fight bravely they might not be  selfless and if they are strong they  might not be merciful usually acting  heroically are doing heroic stuff  because it's in their own self-interest  or they might believe as their  responsibility among other ideals they  hold to be true what are the most  important facets of storytelling that  made Vegeta's eventual antihero turns so  compelling was his slow shift from the  villain role to neutral and eventually  to the good and that right there is  something that set Vegeta apart from  many of the anti heroes of the 80s time  period he developed boy you might be  asking at this point is if Vegeta wasn't  always the antihero how do they change  him into one so the great secret Ana  here  to make the villains and the people  around them worse and less likeable than  the hero themselves so Tony Soprano's  mom is the most miserable horrible human  being  ever conceived on television but so we  love Tony because his mom is just so  awful and we think God poor god no  wonder he turned into a murdering  psychopath the sympathetic character if  there's one surefire way to make your  antihero shine it's by writing a story  where he has to overcome an even more  despicable evil once Vegeta suffered the  defeat at the hands of Goku we are  greeted by this scene while not taking  anything to heart we got our first  indication that Vegeta might not be the  top dog and at one point perhaps  suffered ridicule at the hands of his  superiors that's relatable and that  creeping sense of sympathy is made even  more palpable with the introduction of  Frieza  as I said before an antihero can be  ruthless and merciless he does not need  to be selfless and merciful but the  audience does need to sympathize with  him on some level and he needs to be  juxtaposed to a more severe evil in the  case of Vegeta despite being a seemingly  irredeemable character when he was first  introduced slowly the story fed us more  and more information about why he is the  way he is how his allies see him how his  superiors betrayed him and what life  growing up in subjugation must have been  like  [Music]  the key to connecting an anti-hero to  the audience is sympathy their actions  and moral standing might be questionable  but when the audience can empathize with  the character then they are also able to  accept and understand why he is the way  he is on top of that it frees up space  for us to see his redemption er or good  sight more clearly the beginning of this  for Vegeta was unknown ik his escapades  ducky and weaving around freezes men as  he tried to get the dragonballs for  himself was always a joy to watch and it  was so because we knew he was the lesser  of two evils and had a common enemy with  our heroes which led to this  [Music]  that might be a fun moment to look back  on but it was also the very first time  Vegeta's actions coincided with what the  main cast of good guys wanted and from  that moment on Vegeta had cemented  himself as an antihero  fighting albeit poor growingly alongside  the rest of the Earth's fighters even  going so far as to heal Goku before the  impending Frieza attack and all of that  played into another aspect of Vegeta  that made his antihero so successful and  more importantly compelling he is an  active character that drives the story  forward if you have a full character  that's real that you've created and then  you put something in front of them in a  way they will begin to write the movie  because you know how they're gonna react  to whatever you put in front of them  because they're now these dimensional  characters as opposed to let's have this  happen let's have this happen they said  this happened and we'll just make sure  that they do these things that I've  gained and I think that's what creates a  feeling of artifice or you've seen it  all before or boring I think Vegeta is a  compelling character because instead of  waiting for the story to happen to him  which would be a reactive thing to do  Vegeta the character takes action and  tries to manipulate the odds to his  favor because of this character trait  Vegeta has caused many many terrible  things to happen as well as the good  that I mentioned before regardless  because his character has an active  approach to the story we the audience  find him extremely interesting to watch  because he is the one that moves the  plot forward and that is immensely  compelling opposites attract interest  whenever things get stale in a medium  the antihero has frequently been the  tool a new generation of artists used to  escape the traps the mediums fallen into  and it makes sense in a really  reductionist way the antihero is simply  a rebellion against whatever the medium  has established as a norm the  introduction of characters like piccolo  and Vegeta was a rebellion against what  had become the norm  Dragonball was the story of Goku until  he became a man but the story of Goku as  a man needed to be different and so  Akira Toriyama renamed this section of  the series onward Dragon Ball Z to  reflect that it was coming to an end and  to represent a change in the direction  this series was taking and  reflect that change the main character  introduced during the initial arc of Z  was Vegeta this is a phenomenon that is  observed in every single instance a  certain genre gains a large amount of  notoriety for a long period of time  crime dramas were once all the rage and  so to subvert the trope of having the  good guy protagonist to the genre the  antihero Walter White was created a  sympathetic complicated character that  was doing the wrong thing for all the  right reasons  superhero films now thrive in the  box-office and so it became the perfect  time to have a movie like Deadpool  become a worldwide success now I am by  no means saying that Deadpool was  successful is just because of its  subversion of our expectations the film  was also very well written and shot  however what I am saying is that when a  certain genre becomes stale is  inevitably gives rise to an antihero in  that genre the show turns on R was  inundated with good boys like Goku and  so added an edge into the mix as a main  character giving rise to a whole new  kind of shown in antihero whether or not  Vegeta was the first in manga doesn't  really matter he is the one that has  stood the test of time as the example  don't believe me how many times have you  heard someone say that so and so is the  Vegeta  of that show it might be due to their  moral complexity or even their choices  mirroring our own but anti-heroes for  one reason or another are intensely  interested we earnestly root for their  Redemption and salivate at the thought  of them paying for their mistakes Vegeta  is an antihero that has stood the test  of time when faced with moral Paragons  like Gohan and krillen or morally grey  characters like son Goku we the audience  understand and sympathize with the  choices and actions of a great antihero  like Vegeta speaking on the rise of the  antihero H Erik bender wrote anti-heroes  liberate us they reject social  constraints and expectations imposed  upon us anti-heroes give our grievances  a voice they make us feel like something  right is being done even if it's legally  wrong anti-heroes do things were afraid  to do they are who they are and they do  as they want without apology and I gotta  say for 30 minutes every week watching  Dragon Ball Z we lived vicariously  through him without apology  thank you so much for watching  [Music]  you
 

